  [1m[38;5;153mindex.html[39m changes[0m — one line. [38;5;153m<script src="settings.js"></script>[39m before the existing [38;5;153mapp.js[39m tag. Load order is the
  contract here; that's the cost of the global approach.

  [1mTwo assumptions I invented and need you to correct:[0m the staging hostname ([38;5;153mstaging.example.com[39m →
  [38;5;153mapi-staging.example.com[39m) and the local API port ([38;5;153mlocalhost:3000[39m). Production is real, derived from the existing
  constant. Local detection covers [38;5;153mlocalhost[39m, [38;5;153m127.0.0.1[39m, and the empty hostname you get from [38;5;153mfile://[39m.

  [1mSection 2: Error handling and testing[0m

  [1mUnknown hostname → production.[0m A host not in the table falls through to production rather than throwing. This is
  deliberate: preview deploys and unfamiliar hosts should get a working page, not a blank one. The tradeoff is that a
  typo'd staging hostname silently talks to production — acceptable because the environment name is exposed on
  [38;5;153mAppSettings.environment[39m, so it's visible rather than hidden.

  [1mMissing settings → loud failure.[0m If [38;5;153msettings.js[39m didn't load, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m throws immediately with [38;5;153m"settings.js must be [39m
  [38;5;153mloaded before app.js"[39m rather than letting [38;5;153mundefined[39m propagate into a request URL. A config bug should break at load,
  not at the first login attempt.

  [1mTesting[0m is the open item. The repo has no test runner, no linter, and no build step — [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has zero
  dependencies. [38;5;153mdetectEnvironment(hostname)[39m is the one piece with real logic worth testing (pure function, hostname in,
  environment name out), but there's nowhere to put a test right now.

